{:frontmatter
 {:id "adr-2607032500-wasm-verification-standard"
  :title "ADR-2607032500: JVM verification is insufficient for kami-engine-clj/kami-genre-base-systems -- real WASM execution is the standard"
  :status "accepted"
  :doc_type "adr"
  :topic "kami-engine-clj-wasm-verification"
  :authoritative true
  :last_verified "2026-07-03"
  :authoritative_for
  ["kotoba.engine-clj's JVM test suite (`clojure -M:test`) passing is NOT sufficient
    evidence that compiled game logic is correct -- the i64.const 32-bit truncation bug
    (below) only manifested under ClojureScript's numeric semantics and was invisible to
    every JVM test this session ran, including the 51/51-green suite that predated and
    postdated the bug's presence in the codebase"
   "the standard verification bar for kami-engine-clj-compiled game logic going forward is:
    compile via the real CLJS/ClojureScript path (not JVM), AND actually instantiate+run
    the resulting .wasm bytes via a real WASM host (kami-script-runtime-rs's wasmtime/wasmi,
    or a real browser's WebAssembly.instantiate) -- structural validation
    (`wasm-tools validate`) alone is also insufficient, since it only confirms well-formed
    WASM, not semantically-correct WASM"
   "kotoba-lang/kami-genre-base-systems' `scripts/verify/` (added this session, commit
    `2c4c092`) implements this two-stage standard (CLJS compile -> kami-host execution) and
    is the reference implementation other kami-engine-clj-consuming projects should follow"
   "`author.clj` running successfully (producing a valid scene.edn) proves NOTHING about
    whether the corresponding `logic.clj` compiles or runs -- these are two entirely
    separate paths (datalevin->scene.edn vs. logic.clj->codegen->wasm) and this session
    spent most of its game-development work verifying only the former for
    kami-genre-base-systems' 18 genres, letting 16/18 real compile failures go undetected
    until this ADR's investigated section's explicit logic.clj compile sweep"]
  :related
  ["CLAUDE.md"
   "90-docs/adr/2607032100-consolidate-engine-into-kami-engine-clj.md"
   "90-docs/adr/2607032400-network-isekai-primary-consolidation.md"
   "kotoba-lang/kami-engine PR #95 (fix/cljs-i64-const-32bit-truncation, merged a47f2623)"
   "gftdcojp/network-isekai issue #49 (closed)"
   "kotoba-lang/kami-genre-base-systems@cafb82e (16-genre defatom-read fix)"
   "kotoba-lang/kami-genre-base-systems@2c4c092 (scripts/verify/)"]
  :supersedes []
  :superseded_by []}

 :problem
 {:source "session continuation of ADR-2607032400's network-isekai integration work: the
           user asked to raise game quality via a self-paced /loop, which surfaced two
           real, previously-undetected defects, then explicitly redirected verification
           methodology mid-session: 'jvm じゃなくて kotoba wasm で動いてほしい' (I want it to
           run on kotoba-wasm, not JVM)"
  :defect_1
  {:what "gftdcojp/network-isekai#49/#72's 'unreproduced' null-byte tag-string bug"
   "symptom" "entity tag strings (e.g. \"player\", \"shiro-pico\", \"ghost\") decoded with
              a large null-byte prefix before the correct content, when the compiler's
              output was actually instantiated+run in a real browser (headless Chromium) --
              but a Node.js-only WebAssembly.instantiate test of the same bytes could NOT
              reproduce it, creating a false 'inconclusive/unreproducible' status that
              stood for one full session iteration"
   "root_cause" "kotoba.engine-clj.codegen/emit-str packs a 64-bit string handle as
                 `(abs << 32) | len` via bit-shift-left/bit-or. Under ClojureScript these
                 compile to JavaScript's native 32-bit bitwise operators, which ToInt32-
                 coerce their operand FIRST -- `abs << 32` silently becomes `abs << 0` =
                 `abs`, discarding the high bits entirely. Correct on the JVM, where
                 Clojure's bitwise ops operate on full 64-bit longs -- this is exactly why
                 `clojure -M:test` (51/51 green throughout) never caught it, and why a
                 Node.js smoke test that happened to only exercise values within Node's
                 own numeric edge cases initially seemed not to reproduce it either (the
                 actual repro required the SAME kind of large packed handle a real browser
                 execution against real game data naturally produces). Fixing emit-str
                 alone exposed an identical second-layer instance of the same bug class in
                 wasm_bytes.cljc's sleb128/uleb128 (bit-and/bit-shift-right/
                 unsigned-bit-shift-right also truncate to 32 bits under CLJS)"
   "fix" "replaced the large-operand bitwise operations with arithmetic (+/*/mod/quot, via
          a new `shr7` floored-division helper) in both call sites -- correct on both JVM
          and CLJS since JS doubles are exact for these magnitudes (well under 2^53) and
          JVM longs are exact regardless. Values that provably stay within 32 bits (the
          LEB128 continuation byte itself, masked to [0,127]) keep their existing bitwise
          calls unchanged, since those were never the bug"
   "landed" "kotoba-lang/kami-engine PR #95, merged a47f2623dfd6c1888c902fccd3f68a0a7ed2a75.
             Re-verified via real headless-Chromium execution of both network-isekai's
             tanememi and shiro-pico games post-fix: tag strings decode correctly, ghost
             entities show real varying positions across multiple spawns"}
  :defect_2
  {:what "16 of kami-genre-base-systems' 18 genre base systems had never actually been
          compiled, at all, on any platform, despite every genre's author.clj having run
          successfully all session"
   "root_cause" "the maturity pass (adding combo counters, win/lose conditions, currency,
                 escalating alert levels, etc. to deepen each genre's core loop) read
                 `defatom` values via a bare symbol reference (e.g. `(+ combo 1)`), but
                 kotoba.engine-clj's codegen requires the explicit `(atom-val name)`
                 accessor for reads -- a bare symbol is only a valid codegen target for
                 `set-atom!`'s write side. `sports` additionally used `-`/`<` on f32 values
                 where the compiler requires the f32-specific `-f`/`<f` operators. Every
                 prior verification pass this session ran `author.clj` (the
                 datalevin -> scene.edn data-authoring path) and separately, manually
                 code-reviewed `logic.clj` against a proven-vocabulary checklist -- neither
                 step ever actually invoked `codegen/compile` on the file, so this was a
                 pure compile-time error hiding behind two verification methods that both,
                 independently, happened not to exercise the actual compile step"
   "fix" "wrapped every value-position defatom reference in `(atom-val ...)` across all 16
          affected files; fixed sports' float-op operators. Re-verified all 18/18 compile
          via `codegen/compile` (JVM first, then per this ADR's methodology correction,
          all 18 also re-verified via the real CLJS path + actual wasmtime execution
          through kami-script-runtime-rs's kami-host, not just structural validation)"
   "landed" "kotoba-lang/kami-genre-base-systems@cafb82e (16-file fix) and @2c4c092
             (scripts/verify/, the standing two-stage verification script this ADR
             establishes as the reference pattern)"}}

 :decision
 {:standard "kami-engine-clj-compiled game logic is not 'verified' until it has: (1)
             compiled through the real CLJS/ClojureScript path (kotoba.engine-clj running
             under shadow-cljs or equivalent, not JVM clojure), producing actual .wasm
             bytes, and (2) those bytes have been instantiated and actually RUN via a real
             WASM host (kami-script-runtime-rs's wasmtime or wasmi, or an actual browser's
             WebAssembly.instantiate) for enough ticks to observe genuinely
             mechanic-specific behavior, not just absence-of-crash. JVM `codegen/compile`
             succeeding, or `author.clj` running, or `wasm-tools validate` passing bytes
             structurally, are each individually necessary-but-far-from-sufficient checks
             -- useful as fast preliminary filters, never presented or relied on as
             confirmation of correctness on their own"
  :why_jvm_specifically_insufficient "Clojure's bitwise operators are full-width 64-bit
             `long` operations on the JVM but compile to JavaScript's native 32-bit
             bitwise operators under ClojureScript (per the ECMAScript spec's ToInt32/
             ToUint32 coercion) -- any kami-engine-clj codegen path that packs/unpacks
             values wider than 32 bits via bitwise ops is a live risk of exactly Defect 1's
             bug class recurring silently, invisible to the JVM test suite by
             construction, not by bad luck"
  :why_author_clj_specifically_insufficient "author.clj only exercises the
             datalevin -> Datalog-query -> scene.edn projection (ADR-0036's authoring
             path) -- it has zero code-path overlap with logic.clj's
             parse -> codegen -> wasm-bytes compilation. A game whose author.clj runs
             perfectly can have a logic.clj that has never successfully compiled, as
             Defect 2 demonstrated for 16 of 18 genres simultaneously"
  :reference_implementation "kotoba-lang/kami-genre-base-systems' `scripts/verify/`
             (deps.edn + shadow-cljs.edn + verify/core.cljs + run-verify.sh) implements
             this exact two-stage standard and is the pattern other kami-engine-clj
             consumers (gftdcojp/network-isekai's own game additions, any future
             kami-app-{game} crates) should replicate rather than reinvent"
  :not_wired_to_ci "the verification script exists and was run manually end-to-end
             (18/18 compile, 18/18 execute) during development, but is NOT wired into
             GitHub Actions CI -- `gftdcojp`'s org-level GitHub Actions setting remains
             disabled (confirmed multiple times this session, most recently against
             network-isekai directly), which is outside this session's control and
             requires an org admin. This is a known, previously-reported, unresolved
             blocker, not a new gap introduced here"}

 :consequences
 {:immediate_fixes "both defects are fixed and merged/committed; network-isekai's live
                     browser execution runs correctly end-to-end with zero Rust; all 18
                     kami-genre-base-systems genres are confirmed to actually run, not
                     merely appear to be authored correctly"
  :standing_process_change "any future work on kami-engine-clj itself, or on any
                             kami-engine-clj-consuming game/genre-system repo, should run
                             (or extend) the scripts/verify/-pattern two-stage check before
                             claiming a compile-level change is safe -- JVM-only test
                             passage is no longer sufficient grounds for that claim in this
                             codebase"
  :discovery_cost_acknowledged "this defect class went undetected through this session's
                                 entire earlier compiler-authoring and genre-system-building
                                 work (multiple forks, ~600K+ tokens each) because the
                                 verification methodology itself had this gap -- the fix is
                                 cheap (one helper function, one accessor-wrapping pass) but
                                 the exposure window was the full session until real
                                 browser/WASM execution was finally exercised end-to-end"}

 :alternatives
 [{:option "keep JVM `clojure -M:test` as the sole/primary compiler-correctness gate,
            treat CLJS/browser execution as an optional final smoke test"
   :rejected_because "this was literally the status quo that let Defect 1 ship
                       undetected through a fully-green 51/51 JVM test suite for as long
                       as it existed in the codebase -- demonstrated insufficient by this
                       ADR's own investigation, not a hypothetical concern"}
  {:option "treat `wasm-tools validate` (structural byte validity) as sufficient
            verification once real .wasm bytes are produced, without requiring actual
            execution"
   :rejected_because "Defect 1's corrupted bytes were structurally valid WASM (any 4-byte
                       sequence is a structurally valid f32/i64 immediate) -- validation
                       cannot distinguish semantically-wrong-but-well-formed bytes from
                       correct ones; only actual execution (observing real, mechanic-
                       specific behavior) can"}
  {:option "wire scripts/verify/ into GitHub Actions CI now, matching this session's
            earlier attempt for the (now-archived) isekai-network repo"
   :rejected_because "`gftdcojp` org-level GitHub Actions remains disabled, confirmed
                       multiple times this session against multiple repos in the org --
                       not something this session can resolve; the script exists and is
                       ready to wire in the moment that org-level blocker clears"}]}
